Copyright (c) 2024-2026 Andreas Christ. All rights reserved.

ThinForge is a composite software distribution. Different components are
subject to different licenses, as set out below. The authoritative and
complete compliance statement is `THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md`; this file is
the top-level summary.


1. Proprietary ThinForge Components
-----------------------------------

The following components are proprietary software, copyright Andreas
Christ, and are NOT licensed under the GNU General Public License:

  * The Rust backend and all crates under `crates/`
    (including, without limitation, `thinforge-api`, `thinforge-core`,
    `thinforge-services`).
  * The TypeScript / Vue frontend under `frontend/`.
  * The Go agent under `agent-go/`.
  * All Ansible playbooks under `ansible/` and `quellinfos/ansible-*/`,
    except where a playbook explicitly declares a different license.
  * All container build orchestration, shell scripts, Dockerfiles,
    compose files, documentation, and design assets authored by
    ThinForge, located outside `quellinfos/` and `buildsources/`.
  * Database schemas, migrations, and configuration templates authored
    by ThinForge.
  * The license-issuing tooling under `LizenzTool/`.

These proprietary components are licensed to the customer under a
separate commercial End User License Agreement (EULA). No license is
granted to these components by this file or by the presence of any
GPL-licensed component in the same distribution.

In the absence of a separate EULA, no rights are granted beyond those
automatically conferred by the jurisdiction in which the software is
received.


2. Third-Party Components under the GNU General Public License
---------------------------------------------------------------

This distribution includes third-party components licensed under the
GNU General Public License, Version 2 ("GPL v2") or Version 3
("GPL v3"). These components remain under their respective GPL and
nothing in the proprietary EULA of ThinForge reduces the rights the
GPL grants you with respect to these components.

Directly integrated components shipped in our container images:

  * Partclone      — https://partclone.org/          (GPL v2, in images)
  * EZIO           — https://github.com/tjjh89017/ezio (GPL v2, in images)

Components orchestrated but not distributed by ThinForge the vendor:

  * Clonezilla     — https://clonezilla.org/          (GPL v2; downloaded
                     by customer at install time)
  * DRBL           — https://drbl.org/                (GPL v2; reference
                     only, not shipped)

Additional GPL v2, GPL v3, LGPL, and permissively-licensed packages
are installed from Alpine Linux community repositories into
ThinForge's container images (e.g. `qemu`, `btrfs-progs`, `dnsmasq`,
`grub`, `parted`, `coreutils`, `bash`, `wget`, `ntfs-3g`, `udpcast`,
`libtorrent-rasterbar`). The authoritative list is maintained in
`THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md`.

The complete texts of GPL v2 and GPL v3 are provided in
`LICENSES/GPL-2.0.txt` and `LICENSES/GPL-3.0.txt`. Per-component
metadata, upstream sources, and corresponding source code locations
are documented in `LICENSES/` and summarised in
`THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md`.

A written offer for the corresponding source code of every GPL v2
component distributed in any ThinForge binary release, valid for
three (3) years from the date of that distribution, is provided in
`WRITTEN_OFFER.md`.


3. Interaction Between ThinForge and GPL Components
----------------------------------------------------

The proprietary ThinForge components interact with the GPL v2
components exclusively through process boundaries:

  * as separate executables invoked as child processes (Partclone),
  * as independent operating systems booted on separate machines
    (Clonezilla Live, booted via PXE),
  * as separate daemons addressed over network sockets (EZIO via
    gRPC),
  * and as separate processes where DRBL is used at all.

The ThinForge proprietary code does not link against, statically
combine with, or share address space with any GPL-licensed component.
Accordingly, the proprietary ThinForge components are not "derivative
works" of the GPL components under Section 2 of the GPL v2.


